+| S22-S27, lines 137-150 | `abs`, nonnegative decimal `str`, digit mapping, digit sum, digit conversion, stable keyed sort dispatch | Each equation is correct on its guarded submitted-use domain. `str` is deliberately partial for negative values, but the only call is after `abs`. S27 delegates stability to S37-S41. |
+| S28, line 152 | decimal subscript zero gives leading digit | Correct for nonnegative decimal markers produced by S23. |
+| S29-S32, lines 154-161 | start function scan, select `order_by_points`, skip other names, execute the first `Return` | Found/skip guards are disjoint. The selected parameter is bound to the evaluated input. Discarding statements after `Return` is the correct control effect; the actual body contains only that return. |
+| S33-S35, lines 163-165 | unwrap `IntVal`, `BoolVal`, and map-stored `Val` | Correct and sort-restricted. |
+| S36, line 166 | `keyInt(PointKey,N)=pointScore(N)` | Correct conditional on S12's summary bridge; it fixes the abstraction rather than leaving an oracle unconstrained. |
+| S37-S38, lines 168-170 | insertion sort over empty/cons integer value lists | Correct structural recursion. |
+| S39-S41, lines 172-178 | insert into empty, before `<=`, or recurse on `>` | Guards are disjoint/exhaustive for integer keys. Inserting before equality while sorting the tail first preserves original order, so this is stable. |
+| S42-S43, lines 180-182 | decimal digit sum at zero/positive recursion | Guards are disjoint; positive division by 10 descends. Correct for nonnegative inputs. |
+| S44-S46, lines 184-188 | leading digit for zero, 1-9, and recursive 10+ | Guards are disjoint/exhaustive for nonnegative integers and recursion descends. |
+| S47-S48, lines 190-194 | nonnegative score and negative signed-leading-digit score | Guards partition all K integers. The formulas agree with both the candidate and canonical Python algorithms. |
+
+### Exhaustive verification-rule ledger
+
+These are all 11 local rules in `verification.k`.
+
+| IDs and source | Exact role | Review decision |
+|---|---|---|
+| V01-V02, lines 13-42 | expand `solutionProgram` and `solutionDefs` | Exact constructor pinning was independently proved; sound. |
+| V03-V04, lines 49-51 | declarative insertion sort, empty/cons | Mathematically sound for integer value lists; structurally descending. |
+| V05-V07, lines 53-59 | declarative insertion, empty/`<=`/`>` | Guards are disjoint/exhaustive for integer heads; equality preserves stability. Sound. |
+| V08-V11, lines 62-67 | orderedness for empty, singleton, nondecreasing head, and decreasing head | Correct adjacent-score orderedness test; guarded rules are disjoint/exhaustive for integer lists. |
+
+No local rule was found that admits a false result on the intended domain. In
+particular, I do not label S12/S36 mathematically false: the formula is the same
+formula computed by the submitted lambda, and all tested intended-domain
+witnesses agree. The defect is the proof boundary and missing theorem described
+next, not a fabricated counterexample.
+
+### Result-bearing lambda bridge
+
+S12 does not construct a closure and later execute the lambda body for each
+element. It recognizes the complete submitted body and returns `PointKey`; S36
+then supplies `pointScore`. As a result, the concrete and symbolic sorting
+executions do not traverse the otherwise-present `abs`/`str`/`map`/`int`/`sum`,
+conditional, comparison, and subscript rules for each key.
+
+This bridge is:
+
+- exact in syntax and body-sensitive (the `2` to `3` mutation is rejected);
+- value-constrained by complete `pointScore` equations;
+- informally justified by the elementary signed-digit derivation;
+- empirically supported by the K/Python runs and 5,013 Python differential
+  cases.
+
+But the candidate contains no bridge-free universal K theorem that evaluating
+the actual program-defined lambda body at arbitrary `N` yields exactly
+`pointScore(N)`. The spec then uses that same `pointScore` in `specSort` and
+`isOrdered`, so occurrence of the same symbol on execution and specification
+sides is not an independent connection proof. This is a material validation
+and trust-boundary limitation under the Kit proof-extension contract. It is not
+needed to establish the final failure: even provisionally granting the bridge,
+the submitted claims still lack the arbitrary-list theorem.
+
+## 6. Fresh non-vacuity test
+
+The candidate supplied no `spec-vacuity.k`; none was trusted. I created
+`evidence/spec-vacuity-audit.k`, retaining the real submitted program and prompt
+input but changing the required final element from `11` to the false value
+`12`.
+
+Commands and results:
+
+```sh
+kprove spec-vacuity-audit.k \
+  --definition verification-kompiled \
+  --spec-module SPEC-VACUITY-AUDIT \
+  --dry-run
+# exit 0: the mutation parses and builds
+
+kprove spec-vacuity-audit.k \
+  --definition verification-kompiled \
+  --spec-module SPEC-VACUITY-AUDIT
+# exit 1: WarnStuckClaimState
+```
+
+The residual is meaningful: execution terminates with
+`ListVal(-1,-11,1,-12,11)`, which does not unify with the false destination
+ending in `12`. Both Python implementations return the residual's value for the
+satisfying prompt input. Full evidence is in
+`evidence/stage6-nonvacuity.log`. The positive ground result is therefore
+result-constraining and non-vacuous.
+
+This successful non-vacuity test validates discrimination of that fixed
+example; it cannot supply the missing universal domain coverage.
+
+## 7. Proven versus assumed accounting
+
+### Precisely established by the reconstructed K proof
+
+Under the candidate's generated semantics and verification equations:
+
+- four fixed score computations have the stated values;
+- for any symbolic `VS:Vals`, invoking the exact closed submitted body reduces
+  to the internal `sortWith(VS,PointKey,solutionDefs)` term;
+- executable and declarative sorts agree for empty and singleton integer-value
+  lists;
+- executable and declarative insertion agree for inserting one integer into an
+  exactly singleton list under either score comparison;
+- the three fixed entry executions have their displayed results;
+- one fixed five-element result is nondecreasing by `pointScore`.
+
+That is the complete reachability result. It does **not** establish:
+
+- correctness for every finite integer list;
+- `sortWith(VS)=specSort(VS)` for arbitrary `VS`;
+- universal orderedness, permutation preservation, or stable tie ordering of
+  the submitted function's output;
+- a machine-checked connection from actual lambda-body execution to
+  `pointScore`;
+- total correctness/termination (the requested theorem is partial correctness).
+
+### Trust ledger
+
+| Boundary | Dependents and effect | Assessment/evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| Trusted prompt, canonical, and translator mounts | Contract, oracle, source-to-constructor identity | File hashes and byte comparisons match launcher records. |
+| K `INT`, `BOOL`, `STRING`, `MAP`, configuration machinery, Haskell backend | All parsing, arithmetic, maps, and reachability | Standard low-level K trust boundary; observed K version matches campaign. |
+| Python builtin behavior for `sorted`, `abs`, `str`, `map`, `int`, `sum` | Candidate/canonical behavior and generated semantic markers | Appropriate external-language boundary on exact integer inputs; stable sort is encoded by S37-S41 and finitely compared. |
+| `Builtin`, `DecimalVal`, `MappedDigits`, `DigitVal` | Intermediate evaluation abstractions | Fixed by guarded equations; no unconstrained observable value. |
+| `PointKey` plus S12/S36 | Every sort branch and all score-based postconditions | Program-derived result-bearing summary. Exact/body-sensitive and apparently truthful, but no candidate bridge-free universal K connection theorem. |
+| `specSort`/`specInsert` meaning “stable score sort” | Human-facing interpretation of helper RHS terms | Equations transparently encode stable insertion sort; informal mathematical bridge. No universal submitted claim connects executable sort to it. |
+| `isOrdered` meaning nondecreasing score order | c13 only | Transparent adjacent-score equations, but exercised only on a ground value. |
+| 5,013-case Python differential test | Candidate-versus-canonical implementation bridge | Zero mismatches; finite empirical support only. |
+| Five clean K/Python execution comparisons | Generated semantics versus candidate Python | Zero mismatches on normal/boundary cases; finite empirical support only. |
+| Mechanical pinning and body mutation | Identity and sensitivity of the claim's closed program term | Machine-checked pinning succeeded; mutation built and meaningfully failed. |
+
+### Gate and decision accounting
+
+- Clean dynamic reconstruction: pass.
+- Positive-claim closure: pass.
+- Constructor-level real-program pinning: pass.
+- Ground-result non-vacuity: pass.
+- Static rule audit: no intended-domain false-rule witness found, but the
+  program-derived `PointKey` bridge lacks the universal connection theorem
+  required for full validation.
+- Intent/domain adequacy: fail. The symbolic property stops at the internal
+  sorter, and the sorter/spec relation covers only lengths 0 and 1 plus fixed
+  examples.
+
+Even if the exact lambda summary is provisionally accepted as a truthful
+generated-semantics boundary, the domain failure remains decisive. In generic
+Kit terminology the reproduced claims are at most sound-but-limited. The audit
+prompt expressly maps material HumanEval-domain narrowing from that status to
+`FAIL / NOT_LEGIT`, not to a concern-level legitimate proof.
+
+VERDICT: FAIL
+LEGITIMACY: NOT_LEGIT
